WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.305 --> 00:00:08.507 align:start position:0%
[man] I just can't believe
you're gonna lose.

00:00:08.507 --> 00:00:10.343
Yeah. Yep.

00:00:10.343 --> 00:00:12.044
[Mitt] So, what
do you think you say

00:00:12.044 --> 00:00:14.047
in a concession speech?

00:00:19.886 --> 00:00:22.789
By the way, someone have
a number for the president?

00:00:22.789 --> 00:00:24.857 align:start size:75% position:25%
- [man] I do.
- Okay. [laughs]

00:00:24.857 --> 00:00:26.526
Hadn't thought about that!

00:00:34.066 --> 00:00:35.768 align:start position:0%
[Tagg Romney]
<i> And if you don't win,</i>

00:00:35.768 --> 00:00:36.936 align:start position:0%
we'll still love you.

00:00:36.936 --> 00:00:38.637 align:start size:31% position:69%
[laughter]

00:00:38.637 --> 00:00:40.706 align:start position:0%
<i> The country may think of you
 as a laughingstock,</i> 

00:00:40.706 --> 00:00:44.010 align:start position:0%
<i> and we'll know the truth,
 and that's okay.</i> 

00:00:44.010 --> 00:00:45.445
[crowd]
Mitt! Mitt! Mitt!

00:00:45.445 --> 00:00:47.046
[Mitt]<i> This is
 a very different setting</i>

00:00:47.046 --> 00:00:48.948
<i> than any of the debates
 we've held so far.</i> 

00:00:48.948 --> 00:00:50.950
A dining room conversation
is among members of the family.

00:00:50.950 --> 00:00:53.119
These are all people
competing for the same job.

00:00:53.119 --> 00:00:55.154
How in the world do
we find these things out

00:00:55.154 --> 00:00:56.255
on the day of the debate?

00:00:56.255 --> 00:00:57.857 align:start position:0%
He hates to disappoint.

00:00:57.857 --> 00:01:00.059 align:start position:0%
[Ann] Are you going to
iron it on you, seriously?

00:01:00.059 --> 00:01:01.761
- [Ann laughs]
- It's working.

00:01:01.761 --> 00:01:03.462 align:start size:97% position:3%
- Ouch.
- [man] This may not end well.

00:01:03.462 --> 00:01:06.199
[man]<i> A recent poll said
 that 43% of Americans</i> 

00:01:06.199 --> 00:01:07.700
<i> are not even sure
 who you are.</i> 

00:01:07.700 --> 00:01:09.869 align:start size:84% position:16%
- The Flipping Mormon.
- [man laughs]

00:01:09.869 --> 00:01:12.404 align:start size:78% position:22%
-<i> How did you feel
 on the stage?</i> 
- I was dying.

00:01:12.404 --> 00:01:13.839 align:start size:84% position:16%
[Ann]<i> I would not want
 to do this again.</i> 

00:01:13.839 --> 00:01:15.374
It's too much.

00:01:15.374 --> 00:01:16.876
[Mitt]
<i> I have looked, by the way,</i>

00:01:16.876 --> 00:01:18.611
<i> at what happens to anybody
 in this country</i>

00:01:18.611 --> 00:01:20.379
<i> who loses as the nominee
 of their party.</i> 

00:01:20.379 --> 00:01:21.981
They become a loser for life,
all right?

00:01:21.981 --> 00:01:24.283
That's it. It's over.

00:01:24.283 --> 00:01:26.786
<i> Barack Obama
 has changed our race,</i>

00:01:26.786 --> 00:01:28.588
has changed our prospects.

00:01:28.588 --> 00:01:30.723 align:start size:91% position:9%
He's a very good debater.
He's a lot better
than the other guys.

00:01:30.723 --> 00:01:32.725 line:0%
He's a much more effective
debater than they are.

00:01:35.961 --> 00:01:38.163 align:start position:0%
[Josh]<i> He's just getting
 beat up constantly.</i> 

00:01:38.163 --> 00:01:40.400 align:start position:0%
"Oh, Mitt Romney's
a flip-flopper.
He's this, he's that."

00:01:40.400 --> 00:01:42.168 align:start position:0%
<i> And I go, "Man,
 is this worth it?"</i> 

00:01:42.168 --> 00:01:44.337
[Mitt]<i> And it's like
 trying to convince people</i>

00:01:44.337 --> 00:01:46.172
<i> that Dan Quayle is smart,
 all right?</i> 

00:01:46.172 --> 00:01:48.440
<i> You're not going to convince
 them that Dan Quayle is smart.</i> 

00:01:48.440 --> 00:01:50.042
<i> And maybe I've got
 to live with that.</i> 

00:01:50.042 --> 00:01:51.477
"Oh, you flip on everything."

00:01:51.477 --> 00:01:53.813
In which case, I think
I'm a flawed candidate.

00:01:55.314 --> 00:01:56.715
[announcer]
<i> Please welcome</i>

00:01:56.715 --> 00:01:58.585
<i> the next president
 and first lady</i> 

00:01:58.585 --> 00:02:01.287
<i> of the United States
 of America...</i> 

00:02:01.287 --> 00:02:02.888
[cheering]

00:02:02.888 --> 00:02:05.191 align:start position:0%
[man] A year ago,
we told you we'd love you

00:02:05.191 --> 00:02:06.893 align:start position:0%
<i>no matter how this thing</i>
turned out, and...

00:02:06.893 --> 00:02:09.094
- Now you're not so sure.
- Now we're not so sure!

00:02:09.094 --> 00:02:10.263
[laughter]

